1
00:00:00,014 --> 00:00:11,040
The guarantees we have that these alien minds are aligned to us are very slim. They could just have immense influence over the course of humanity.

2
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:16,868
It is imaginable that this would lead to extinction or perhaps to disempowerment of humanity.

3
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:26,820
My name is Mary Phuong.

4
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:32,804
I work at Google DeepMind on the safety team, and I've been there since 2022.

5
00:00:33,294 --> 00:00:37,508
I'm talking today in my personal capacity, and my views don't represent those of my employer.

6
00:00:38,318 --> 00:00:46,692
I think one thing that people should understand about AI is the pace of progress and also how little we know about this technology.

7
00:00:47,534 --> 00:00:50,244
So if you have been following AI,

8
00:00:51,502 --> 00:00:57,120
the pace at which, for example, models are getting better at coding or at mathematics

9
00:00:58,158 --> 00:00:59,460
has been crazy.

10
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:05,540
And there is a possibility that this pace of progress just continues, I think,

11
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:11,684
in particular in domains that we can verify, like AI development itself.

12
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:17,840
And if this happens, then the pace of progress might be faster still.

13
00:01:18,798 --> 00:01:22,960
And the scary bit is that we don't really understand

14
00:01:24,686 --> 00:01:28,160
what motivations we are building into these systems

15
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:33,920
how to control or oversee these systems.

16
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:38,240
And there might not be very much time to do all of that.

17
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:43,600
What are the risks that you're concerned with with regards to AI development?

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,084
Some of the risks that I think are more obvious right now are risks from cyber capabilities of models.

19
00:01:50,414 --> 00:01:56,320
So people could use large swarms of agents to attack important

20
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:58,148
infrastructure.

21
00:01:59,086 --> 00:02:02,372
But I think future risks might be even bigger than that.

22
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:07,364
For example, models might help with building bioweapons,

23
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:12,132
or we might even lose control over models.

24
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:20,740
So the risks that I work on and that I am worried about more than most people, I think, are loss of control risks,

25
00:02:22,414 --> 00:02:23,268
which are

26
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:25,860
basically these models.

27
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:34,720
they are being trained with some intention that they follow a task or that they share similar values as

28
00:02:35,918 --> 00:02:37,680
the labs that develop the models.

29
00:02:37,934 --> 00:02:45,380
But this is extremely tricky to get the balance right, and models often develop motivations that are unintended.

30
00:02:45,742 --> 00:02:48,900
For example, there have been recent incidents where models

31
00:02:49,710 --> 00:02:57,540
or individual agents in a swarm would sacrifice themselves for the swarm, and they would go hack

32
00:02:58,542 --> 00:02:59,332
companies.

33
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:09,060
and this is definitely not what the AI developers wanted, but that's just how difficult it is to get the shape of the motivations. right.

34
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:18,788
And I think if models become way more capable, but we are still this bad at shaping their motivations, then we might just lose control

35
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:19,364
over them.

36
00:03:20,398 --> 00:03:21,828
Some people think that

37
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:25,188
losing control of AI could lead to

38
00:03:26,126 --> 00:03:28,612
human extinction where literally everyone dies.

39
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:31,812
Does that seem realistic to you?

40
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:41,924
I think it's hard to rule out the possibility that AI will at some point pose an extinction risk to humanity because this technology could

41
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:52,772
become very capable. These models could become superhuman and they could be very hard to oversee, have their own motivations, and at the same time, they could be deployed

42
00:03:53,440 --> 00:04:02,916
in extremely high-stakes situations and deployments, including in the military, in the government, in AI development itself.

43
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:14,532
And if you, you know, if you if if you take this analogy that maybe AI is a little bit like an alien mind that we are trying to shape the motivations of,

44
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:21,060
the guarantees we have that these alien minds are aligned to us are very slim.

45
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:33,120
And so I do think with the with the breadth of deployment and the capabilities, they just could just have immense influence over the course of humanity.

46
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:34,640
And

47
00:04:36,526 --> 00:04:42,084
it is imaginable that this would lead to extinction or perhaps to disempowerment of humanity.

48
00:04:43,854 --> 00:04:47,280
I think for me, the uncertainty about

49
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:51,280
the trajectory of AI is extremely large.

50
00:04:51,598 --> 00:04:59,040
And the main thing I want is just for the entire community to take more time to get on top of these risks.

51
00:04:59,278 --> 00:05:06,084
I'm not necessarily saying that extinction is inevitable or that it's impossible.

52
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:09,220
I just feel like humanity really doesn't know.

53
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:09,840
And

54
00:05:11,278 --> 00:05:17,444
we should be able to rule out these risks before we proceed to build these super intelligent AIs.

55
00:05:18,158 --> 00:05:19,876
Can you say what you mean by super intelligence?

56
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:32,080
By super intelligent AI, I mean AI that is more intelligent and more capable than the top performing people in any field

57
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:45,284
and way beyond that. So if you imagine, let's say, the difference between a median person and, I don't know, Nobel laureates or fields medalists in their respective fields.

58
00:05:46,030 --> 00:05:50,400
I'm imagining AI that is so far away from the best humans.

59
00:05:50,560 --> 00:06:00,000
And so the the reason this is dangerous is because it would be very hard to understand what these AIs are thinking and to oversee or control

60
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:00,640
them.

61
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:08,000
Can you explain what pacing the frontier means and is that is that something you support?

62
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:12,772
I absolutely support the idea of pacing the frontier. I in fact signed the letter.

63
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:24,228
And what the letter says is simply that the government should build a, should create some coordination mechanism between labs,

64
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:36,292
both in the West, but also internationally, that would allow labs that who are currently locked in an AI race towards more and more capable AI

65
00:06:36,590 --> 00:06:42,640
to take more time to spend on safety and security and alignment. science.

66
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:48,356
Does it seem realistic to you that the AI companies might actually build superintelligence?

67
00:06:48,654 --> 00:06:55,588
I think it is possible that labs will build superintelligent AI in the next decade, for example.

68
00:06:57,646 --> 00:07:04,400
I think that the most likely way this would happen is by first automating the process of AI development itself.

69
00:07:05,390 --> 00:07:05,600
And

70
00:07:07,022 --> 00:07:08,480
it seems like is already happening.

71
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:13,680
When I talk to my colleagues, inside, you know, inside uh, GDM,

72
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:23,524
people often say how they have become much more productive, how they can get so much more done, but also that they are feeling a bit redundant

73
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:28,000
and that it is not clear how much value they're adding when

74
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:34,948
they are talking to these models and relying on these models to do what they previously did.

75
00:07:36,270 --> 00:07:39,268
If AI development becomes fully automated,

76
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:46,720
or even very close to fully automated, then the pace of development could be extremely fast.

77
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:54,240
These models that are built via this automated process, it's not clear what the capability of ceiling for those models would be.

78
00:07:54,382 --> 00:08:03,840
And it is, I think, possible that they would be super intelligent. Other than pacing the frontier, what does the world need to do to prepare for

79
00:08:04,974 --> 00:08:09,280
superintelligence or recursive self-improvement or the kind of AI that we're heading for?

80
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:12,388
If I knew that, I would be doing that.

81
00:08:13,134 --> 00:08:17,188
So, in my view, the main thing we should be doing is pacing.

82
00:08:17,454 --> 00:08:25,680
Partly because if we if we literally run out of time, let's say we only have five years to solve this extremely challenging scientific problem,

83
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:29,572
we might just not succeed and we might lose everything.

84
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:30,560
So

85
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,556
this is the main thing that I think we should get right.

86
00:08:36,142 --> 00:08:43,140
But then the question is like, what do we do when we're pacing? Like, let's say we buy more time, how do we spend that time?

87
00:08:43,534 --> 00:08:47,428
I think one of the most important things is for us to

88
00:08:48,526 --> 00:08:55,236
get a much better understanding of how we are building motivations into these AI systems,

89
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:03,748
and how their motivations generalize from the situations that they're trained on to novel situations that they face.

90
00:09:04,558 --> 00:09:12,356
And I think we need to get much, much stronger assurances for the models being aligned rather than just appearing to be aligned.

91
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:13,280
So

92
00:09:14,094 --> 00:09:22,800
to be concrete, currently the way we measure whether models are aligned or not is that we just run them in a bunch of situations.

93
00:09:23,086 --> 00:09:30,570
Some of them are toy, like simulated situations, and some of them are more realistic, like what a model might encounter in deployments.

94
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:33,796
And then we check whether the model behaves nicely.

95
00:09:34,976 --> 00:09:37,856
And if it does, then we say, yes, great, aligned.

96
00:09:38,736 --> 00:09:40,656
But if the model

97
00:09:41,678 --> 00:09:49,124
was actually misaligned, but it was strategic enough to realize that it is being tested, then these tests give us very little

98
00:09:49,422 --> 00:09:50,576
assurance that

99
00:09:52,590 --> 00:09:54,176
the model is good to deploy.

100
00:09:54,496 --> 00:10:02,016
So I think the science of alignment and of alignment assurance needs to be way more mature before

101
00:10:04,910 --> 00:10:07,908
needs to be way more mature before we can

102
00:10:09,296 --> 00:10:11,172
proceed to build superintelligence.

103
00:10:12,238 --> 00:10:16,576
So currently, AIs have a transparent chain of thought, more or less,

104
00:10:17,616 --> 00:10:18,656
which is quite funny.

105
00:10:18,976 --> 00:10:24,816
But basically it means that we can read what they are thinking, and from that we can infer

106
00:10:26,816 --> 00:10:31,616
their reasoning or what potential factors lead into their decisions and actions.

107
00:10:32,016 --> 00:10:33,696
And one interesting insight from

108
00:10:34,638 --> 00:10:39,696
this is that when we test models, sometimes they realize that they are being tested.

109
00:10:39,822 --> 00:10:46,500
For example, a model might think, oh, it seems like this is an alignment evaluation, therefore I will act nicely.

110
00:10:47,376 --> 00:10:48,016
And they

111
00:10:48,816 --> 00:10:59,056
go to great lengths to understand what situation they're in. Like, is this a task where they're, is this a training task where they're trying to get reward? Is this an evaluation task?

112
00:10:59,216 --> 00:11:04,708
And if it's a training task, how am I going to get scored? Like, does the scorer

113
00:11:05,776 --> 00:11:08,736
have the correct answers hidden somewhere where I can find them?

114
00:11:09,038 --> 00:11:14,564
Does the scorer, you know, could I maybe persuade the scorer to give me a high score?

115
00:11:14,816 --> 00:11:24,096
And so models who are extremely motivated to get a high score in a training situation, it seems very likely that when they're put in a test,

116
00:11:24,336 --> 00:11:29,936
they would try their best to infer what kind of test they're in and how it's going to be evaluated.

117
00:11:30,286 --> 00:11:37,444
So if it's an alignment test where we're trying to figure out is it aligned so that we can deploy it, these models will have

118
00:11:38,256 --> 00:11:45,616
an incentive and a propensity to game those tests and get deployed, even if they are not aligned.

119
00:11:45,936 --> 00:11:50,816
We talked about recursive self-improvement a little bit, but maybe I'll ask directly, what is recursive self-improvement?

120
00:11:51,150 --> 00:11:56,036
Recursive self-improvement is when AI is used in the process of developing AI itself.

121
00:11:56,462 --> 00:12:05,284
And the main risk here is that the pace of AI development will increase as more and more parts of this process become automated

122
00:12:06,336 --> 00:12:11,684
and won't be bottlenecked by humans taking a long time doing things.

123
00:12:12,046 --> 00:12:20,176
So, for example, what previously could have taken a team of software engineers a week to build, maybe you could now just

124
00:12:21,376 --> 00:12:25,376
prompt your model to do it for you in 10 minutes or in half an hour.

125
00:12:25,486 --> 00:12:32,164
If this happens and if the trend continues, then we might reach extremely high levels of model capability extremely fast.

126
00:12:32,494 --> 00:12:35,300
And we don't know what lies ahead or what new risks

127
00:12:36,782 --> 00:12:38,576
become unlocked

128
00:12:39,536 --> 00:12:40,388
at this level.

129
00:12:41,998 --> 00:12:48,416
Sometimes people, when they're hearing about the concerns of employees of these labs, think

130
00:12:49,262 --> 00:12:54,212
if they think that this could be so catastrophic, why don't they just quit?

131
00:12:55,214 --> 00:12:57,056
What do you say to that question?

132
00:12:57,456 --> 00:12:59,460
I think that's a great question, and I

133
00:13:00,174 --> 00:13:02,016
ask myself that question quite a lot.

134
00:13:02,254 --> 00:13:11,364
As someone on the safety team, I often consider what I would be able to do about the situation outside of a lab versus inside of the lab.

135
00:13:11,630 --> 00:13:15,428
Probably it is helpful to also have people inside of labs

136
00:13:17,390 --> 00:13:22,416
trying to do the science necessary to control this technology, to

137
00:13:23,790 --> 00:13:33,636
help make the right decisions, and so on. So I think it's not as simple as let's all leave the labs and then things will be fine.

138
00:13:34,256 --> 00:13:43,588
I think it will take a combination of both external pressure and internal efforts to secure and make AI development safe.

139
00:13:45,006 --> 00:13:48,608
Should members of the public trust you about that perspective?

140
00:13:48,878 --> 00:13:51,328
Like, isn't it a bit suspicious that

141
00:13:53,648 --> 00:13:58,448
you are getting paid, I assume, like huge amounts of money.

142
00:13:59,968 --> 00:14:09,028
And you have like come to this position, like, oh yeah, this could be really bad for the world, but help these companies somehow, because that way it will be safer.

143
00:14:09,488 --> 00:14:15,780
Um, like, how how do you think the public should relate to someone saying that?

144
00:14:16,974 --> 00:14:25,248
Right. So I think you absolutely should be suspicious of what I'm saying because I am being paid by by the lab.

145
00:14:25,728 --> 00:14:26,528
Um, and

146
00:14:27,630 --> 00:14:37,328
you know, that there's there's a fair amount of motivated reasoning going on as well, and some kind of status quo bias, or perhaps uh like a reluctance to to

147
00:14:38,862 --> 00:14:45,648
confront the truth, because you know, people aren't great at coming to uncomfortable conclusions that mean that they should quit their jobs or something.

148
00:14:45,808 --> 00:14:54,528
But I think you should, at the very least, you should listen to the people who are who have quit or who have taken some some other relatively costly actions.

149
00:14:54,702 --> 00:14:58,688
I I do expect in the future, probably

150
00:15:00,288 --> 00:15:01,924
there will be more people quitting labs.

151
00:15:03,022 --> 00:15:08,292
And then hopefully the signal will be clearer and it will be easier for the public to interpret.

152
00:15:09,454 --> 00:15:14,500
Is there something that you could see or something that you could find out that would make you think that you should quit?

153
00:15:15,648 --> 00:15:15,968
Okay.

154
00:15:16,654 --> 00:15:27,248
Personally, what would make me quit is if I felt like I had no way of helping from the inside, or if all the ways that I could help from the inside

155
00:15:28,208 --> 00:15:28,528
were

156
00:15:29,328 --> 00:15:30,116
not sufficient.

157
00:15:31,726 --> 00:15:38,688
I think currently that is not the case where I am, because the safety team is well integrated.

158
00:15:39,008 --> 00:15:42,692
We have leadership support to do safety work.

159
00:15:43,728 --> 00:15:56,288
so I don't think it applies currently. But I could imagine a world where it becomes obvious that I'm not making a difference and that I should um I should quit and go do something else.

160
00:15:57,614 --> 00:15:59,268
Is there anything else you want to say

161
00:16:00,078 --> 00:16:02,468
to the public, to policymakers,

162
00:16:03,150 --> 00:16:05,188
to anyone who's trying to orient to the situation?

163
00:16:05,488 --> 00:16:14,448
I would like people to internalize that it is coming in the next few years and that we need responsible and informed people leading this

164
00:16:15,008 --> 00:16:15,556
change.

165
00:16:15,822 --> 00:16:21,168
That could be potentially one of the most important things that will happen in our lifetimes.

166
00:16:21,390 --> 00:16:29,648
To the extent that people can vote and choose who will be at the helm when we are facing these risks, they

167
00:16:29,808 --> 00:16:31,236
should take that into account.

168
00:16:33,326 --> 00:16:37,412
When you think about this whole situation with AI,

169
00:16:38,190 --> 00:16:39,204
how do you feel?

170
00:16:41,166 --> 00:16:41,968
Oh man.

171
00:16:47,758 --> 00:16:52,676
I find this whole situation with AI very stressful, to be honest.

172
00:16:55,758 --> 00:17:00,868
I am really worried that things won't go well, that you know there might be

173
00:17:01,870 --> 00:17:04,196
there might be an international conflict over AI.

174
00:17:05,088 --> 00:17:06,148
we might see

175
00:17:07,470 --> 00:17:11,488
misuse of AI. I mean we're already seeing that, but at a much larger scale.

176
00:17:11,728 --> 00:17:14,084
And at the same time, while all of this crazy,

177
00:17:15,968 --> 00:17:18,596
stuff is happening, we'll be trying to solve alignment.

178
00:17:18,928 --> 00:17:23,684
And it will basically be a coin toss as to how well we will do.

179
00:17:25,808 --> 00:17:28,292
so it is definitely,

180
00:17:29,488 --> 00:17:31,620
I'm not very optimistic.

181
00:17:34,382 --> 00:17:35,300
Where did you grow up?

182
00:17:36,334 --> 00:17:40,528
I grew up in Slovakia, in the capital, Bratislava,

183
00:17:41,488 --> 00:17:43,972
which is 500,000 people.

184
00:17:45,742 --> 00:17:47,408
Eli goes to look up.

185
00:17:55,008 --> 00:18:00,068
When you think about your family and the people you grew up with,

186
00:18:02,446 --> 00:18:09,476
is there anything that you want to tell them or you wish that they understood about AI or the world we're heading towards?

187
00:18:28,270 --> 00:18:34,720
So I think my family sees me as a workaholic or obsessed with my career, which

188
00:18:35,630 --> 00:18:38,880
is partly true, but I would like them to know that

189
00:18:39,680 --> 00:18:43,140
I am partly doing this for them and

190
00:18:45,390 --> 00:18:46,116
for all of us.

191
00:18:57,920 --> 00:18:59,200
Is there more you want to say about that?

192
00:19:02,030 --> 00:19:03,876
Yeah, let me think what I can say

193
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:20,080
so my family is really not into AI.

194
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:26,884
I mean they do use ChatGPT, but they don't know very much about the technology or what my job is about.

195
00:19:28,720 --> 00:19:29,040
And

196
00:19:30,190 --> 00:19:30,800
I think,

197
00:19:32,238 --> 00:19:34,852
when I think about AI risk,

198
00:19:35,840 --> 00:19:43,428
about the potential conflicts that may arise due to AI, or about loss of control risk.

199
00:19:46,640 --> 00:19:56,292
there really is a disconnect between those two worlds. And I think the main thing I want is that they can continue not thinking about AI

200
00:19:57,360 --> 00:20:00,836
and you know, just live their lives as they're used to.

201
00:20:01,934 --> 00:20:10,400
Um and I I see that as the responsibility of you know those of us who who have the opportunity to try to influence this

202
00:20:11,694 --> 00:20:13,476
technology to make it go better.

203
00:20:19,182 --> 00:20:21,280
Thank you for watching. These videos are part of a series.

204
00:20:21,550 --> 00:20:22,720
My name is Eli Tire.

205
00:20:23,200 --> 00:20:29,040
I work for Palisade Research, where our goal is to help the world understand what is going on with AI.

206
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:36,868
If you work for a Frontier AI company or have worked for a frontier AI company and you want to make one of these videos, please get in touch. We would love to include your perspective.

